course: books
lesson: 28
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter I, part 4: The River Bank (the Mole grabs the oars, the boat goes over, and a friendship begins)
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Rowing home in the evening sun, the Rat is murmuring poetry to himself. The Mole, full of lunch and pride, thinks rowing looks easy. «Not yet,» smiles the Rat. But the Mole gets jealous, jumps up, grabs the oars — and knocks the Rat off his seat, legs in the air for the second time that day.
explain: The Mole digs at the water, misses it completely, flips over onto the Rat, grabs the side — and Sploosh! The boat turns over. The water is freezing; the Mole sinks, comes up spluttering, sinks again — until a firm paw grabs his neck. The Rat is laughing as he pushes him to shore with the oars under his arms.
explain: The Mole has to trot up and down to dry himself while the Rat rescues the boat and dives for the picnic basket. Ashamed, the Mole says sorry with all his heart. The Rat just says «What's a little wet to a Water Rat?» and invites him to come and live with him, and promises to teach him to row and swim.
explain: The Mole is so touched he cries a little, and the Rat kindly looks the other way. At home there's a warm fire, a dressing-gown and slippers, river stories till supper, and the best bedroom with the River lapping at the window.
explain: That was only the first of many happy summer days. The Mole learns to row and swim, and, with his ear to the reeds, begins to hear what the wind is whispering in them — the «wind in the willows» of the book's title.
fact: The Rat sculled home «murmuring poetry-things over to himself»; the Mole was «very full of lunch, and self-satisfaction, and pride».
fact: «Ratty! Please, I want to row, now!» — «Not yet, my young friend ... wait till you've had a few lessons. It's not so easy as it looks.»
fact: The Mole's «pride began to whisper that he could do it every bit as well»; he seized the sculls and the Rat «fell backwards off his seat with his legs in the air for the second time».
fact: «Stop it, you silly ass!» cried the Rat. «You can't do it! You'll have us over!»
fact: The Mole «missed the surface altogether»; he grabbed the side of the boat, «and the next moment—Sploosh! Over went the boat».
fact: «a firm paw gripped him by the back of his neck. It was the Rat, and he was evidently laughing—the Mole could feel him laughing, right down his arm and through his paw».
fact: The Rat pushed a scull under each of the Mole's arms and swam behind, pushing him to shore, «a squashy, pulpy lump of misery».
fact: «Trot up and down the towing-path as hard as you can, till you're warm and dry again, while I dive for the luncheon-basket.» (a towing-path is a path along a river where horses once pulled boats)
fact: The Mole's apology: «I am very sorry indeed for my foolish and ungrateful conduct ... Indeed, I have been a complete ass, and I know it.» He also says his heart fails him when he thinks «how I might have lost that beautiful luncheon-basket».
fact: The Rat: «That's all right, bless you! What's a little wet to a Water Rat? ... I really think you had better come and stop with me for a little time ... And I'll teach you to row, and to swim». («stop with me» = stay with me)
fact: The Mole «had to brush away a tear or two with the back of his paw. But the Rat kindly looked in another direction».
fact: Cheered up, the Mole gave «some straight back-talk to a couple of moorhens» who were «sniggering» at his «bedraggled» (wet and messy) look.
fact: The Rat's river stories: weirs, floods, leaping pike, «steamers that flung hard bottles—at least bottles were certainly flung, and from steamers, so presumably by them», herons, «night-fishings with Otter, or excursions far a-field with Badger».
fact: The chapter ends: «with his ear to the reed-stems he caught, at intervals, something of what the wind went whispering so constantly among them.»
fact: Old words: «prostrate» = lying flat; «dejected» = very sad; «conduct» = behaviour; «emancipated» = set free; «a-field» = far from home; «You'll have us over» = you'll tip the boat over.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «nesh» (North / Midlands England) = feels the cold easily; «our kid» = my brother or sister; «salty» = bitter, annoyed; «chur» (New Zealand) = thanks; «dreich» (Scotland) = grey and wet; «I'm dead» = that's so funny.
exercise: What is the Rat doing as he rows home? | Murmuring poetry to himself | Singing about ducks and their tails | Eating the leftovers | Telling the Mole about Toad
exercise: What is the Mole full of on the way home? | Lunch, self-satisfaction and pride | Fear of the water | Sadness about his house | Whitewash and dust from the morning's cleaning
exercise: What does the Rat say when the Mole first asks to row? | «Not yet ... wait till you've had a few lessons» | «Yes, of course, here are the sculls, my young friend — off you go, and mind the bank» | «Never» | «Only for sixpence»
exercise: What feeling makes the Mole grab the oars? | Jealousy and pride | Fear that the Rat would fall asleep | Boredom on the river | Kindness to the Rat
exercise: What happens to the Rat when the Mole grabs the sculls? | He falls backwards off his seat, legs in the air | He falls straight into the river with a great splash | He grabs them back | Nothing
exercise: How many times today has the Rat ended up with his legs in the air? | Twice | Only once | Three times | Never
exercise: What does the Rat shout from the bottom of the boat? | «Stop it, you silly ass!» | «Well done, keep going, old fellow!» | «Help! Otter, come quickly!» | «Look ahead, there's the bank!»
exercise: What goes wrong with the Mole's first stroke? | He misses the water altogether | He breaks one of the oars in half on a rock | He hits the Rat | He rows backwards
exercise: What sound does the boat make going over? | «Sploosh!» | «cloop!» | «pop!» | «Poop-poop!»
exercise: How does the Mole feel in the water? | Very cold and very wet | Warm and happy, as he swims about | Proud | Bored
exercise: What is the Rat doing as he grabs the Mole by the neck? | Laughing | Crying softly | Shouting crossly | Singing a song
exercise: How does the Rat get the Mole to shore? | Oars under his arms, pushing from behind | Carrying him on his back like a raft | In the boat | Calling the Otter
exercise: What does the Rat tell the wet Mole to do? | Trot up and down the towing-path | Go home at once to his own house and stay there till morning | Swim about | Sit still
exercise: What does the Rat dive for? | The luncheon-basket | The mustard pot he'd been sitting on all lunch | His hat | The Mole's coat
exercise: What does the Mole call himself in his apology? | «a complete ass» | «a silly fool, and a very bad mole» | «a wicked animal» | «a very good rower»
exercise: What does the Rat say about getting wet? | «What's a little wet to a Water Rat?» | «I hate getting wet more than anything in the world» | «Never again» | «Go home»
exercise: What does the Rat invite the Mole to do? | Come and stay with him for a while | Go and live with Toad at Toad Hall | Leave at once | Find his own boat
exercise: Why does the Rat look «in another direction»? | So the Mole can cry without being watched | He sees the Otter coming back with a fish | He's bored | He sees Toad
exercise: Who giggles at the Mole's «bedraggled appearance»? | A couple of moorhens | The rabbits from the hedge | The Otter, with a fish | Badger, from the Wild Wood
exercise: What does the Rat lend the Mole at home? | A dressing-gown and slippers | A black velvet smoking-suit | A towel | A boat
exercise: Which is NOT one of the Rat's river stories? | A motor-car on the high road | Leaping pike | Night-fishings with Otter | Herons
exercise: Where does the Mole sleep that night? | The best bedroom | The cellar, in the dark | His own home underground | The boat, under a rug
exercise: What does the Mole learn as summer goes on? | To swim and to row | To fly like the swallows | To sail a wager-boat in races | To write poems about ducks
exercise: With his ear to the reed-stems, what does the Mole begin to hear? | What the wind whispers among them | The fish talking under the water to each other | Toad's boat | Bells
test: Mia, bit salty (annoyed): «the Rat taught the Mole to row straight away, he was a natural.» Put it right. | The Rat said «Not yet»; the Mole grabbed the oars and tipped them over | Right: he rowed perfectly all the way home and the Rat praised him | The Mole never asked | The Otter taught him
test: «his pride began to whisper that he could do it every bit as well». What does this mean? | His pride made him think he was as good as the Rat | His pride was actually whispering out loud | He was proud of the Rat | He felt shy
test: The Rat is laughing while he saves the Mole. Is he being cruel? | No: he isn't worried — water is his home | Yes: he wants the Mole to drown, because the Mole tipped his boat over on purpose | Yes: he's mocking him | No: he's crying
test: Why does the Mole say he might have «lost that beautiful luncheon-basket»? | He feels terrible about the trouble | He only cares about food and can think of nothing else, even in the water | He wants more lunch | He's joking
test: What would a less kind friend have done after the boat went over? | Shouted and sent the Mole home | Laughed and invited him to stay, exactly as the Rat did | Bought him a boat | Taught him to swim
test: Leo, proper nesh (feels the cold): «I'd have just sat there shivering». What did the Rat tell the Mole to do instead? | Trot up and down till dry | Sit by the warm fire in the parlour, wrapped up in a big dressing-gown | Jump back in | Sleep
test: «I am very sorry indeed for my foolish and ungrateful conduct.» Why «ungrateful»? | The Rat was kind all day, and the Mole grabbed his oars | He never once said thank you for the lovely lunch the Rat had packed for him | The Rat was mean | He forgot a name
test: The Mole gives «straight back-talk» to the moorhens. What does this show? | His spirits have come back | He is still crying | He is rude to everyone | He loves moorhens
test: The Rat's stories have «steamers that flung hard bottles—at least bottles were certainly flung, and from steamers, so presumably by them». What's the joke? | People on the steamers threw them, not the boats | Steamers really are alive in this book and throw bottles at rats who annoy them | Rats throw bottles | There's no joke
test: Looking back: what did the Rat say at the very start about messing about in boats — «In or out of 'em, it doesn't matter»? How does that come true now? | They end up out of the boat, and the Rat doesn't mind a bit | They never fall in | The Rat stops boating for ever | They buy a new one
test: Looking back: the first time the Rat fell on his back in the boat, whose fault was it? | His own: he crashed while dreaming | The Mole's, for grabbing the oars out of his paws without asking | The Otter's | The rabbits'
test: Looking back: what had the Mole left unfinished at home that very morning? | His spring-cleaning | His supper of bread, cheese and pickles | A poem about ducks | A letter
test: Looking back: in part 3, the Mole packed the basket «without much loss of temper». How is the end of the day different? | His pride makes him tip the boat | He packs the basket all over again, very slowly, without losing his temper | He loses the basket for ever | He stays calm
test: Looking back: how has the Mole changed since he jeered «Onion-sauce!» at the rabbits? | He learns to say sorry and be grateful | He's even ruder now, to the Rat, the moorhens and everyone else on the river | He hasn't changed | He's scared
test: Isla, dreich day out (grey and wet): «the Mole went back to his own house that night.» Is she right? | No: he slept in the Rat's best bedroom | Yes: he went home to finish his spring-cleaning before bed, as he'd promised himself | Yes, with the Otter | No: in the boat
test: ★★★ The book is called «The Wind in the Willows». Where does the title first show up in this chapter? | The Mole hears what the wind whispers among the reeds | The Rat sings «The Wind in the Willows» | Badger's door says it | The Otter's story
test: ★★★ Put in order: (a) the Otter vanishes; (b) the boat goes over; (c) the Rat says «Not yet»; (d) Badger says «H'm! Company». | d, a, c, b | a, d, c, b | d, a, b, c | c, d, a, b
test: ★★★ «This day was only the first of many similar ones». What does «similar» tell us? | The next days were like it — happy, on the river, with friends | Every day the boat tipped over again | The days were all the same, boring | He never saw the river again
exam: Identify the sentiment which impelled the Mole to seize the sculls. | Jealousy and pride | Fear of the deep water | Fatigue after the meal | Gratitude towards his host
exam: Ascertain the means whereby the Rat conveyed the Mole to the bank following the capsize. | A scull beneath each arm, the Rat propelling from behind | The boat, having righted itself | The Otter's assistance | A rope cast from the shore
exam: Select the response which most accurately characterises the Rat's reply to the Mole's apology. | He forgave him and invited him to stay | He declined to forgive him and required that he return forthwith to his own residence | He demanded compensation | He sent him home
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the conclusion of the first chapter? | The Mole learnt to swim and row, and heard the wind in the reeds | The Mole returned to his dwelling and resumed the spring-cleaning which he had abandoned that morning | The Mole departed for the Wide World | The Rat forsook the river
